<Base title="Watching storage in a running database" description="Where PostgreSQL, InnoDB and SQLite report their cache, dirty pages, checkpoints, log and flushes, what ordinary and troubled readings look like, and how to read slow commits, a long restart, a filling log directory or growing history from them.">
  <article class="visual-essay relational-design watching-storage">
    <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Watching storage in a running database</h1><TitleLine type="Companion" version="PostgreSQL 18, with InnoDB (MySQL 8.4) and SQLite" /></header>
<ArticleBody depth={3}>

    <p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/">Storage and durability</a> followed one price change through the buffer pool, the log, a checkpoint and a crash. From the application, almost none of that is visible. A commit waiting on a slow flush just takes longer. A checkpoint writing thousands of pages appears as a few slow requests. A log that can’t be recycled appears as a disk filling up for no obvious reason. Yet each mechanism does report what it is doing, in counters the engine keeps and in lines it writes to its own log. This article reads those reports one mechanism at a time: where to find them in each engine, what an ordinary reading looks like, and what a bad one usually means.</p>
    <p>PostgreSQL 18 is the worked example. The outputs shown come from a real PostgreSQL 18.6 server on a laptop, running the banking workload that ships with its <code>pgbench</code> benchmark tool. They show the shape of each signal, not numbers to expect on your hardware. Sizes are in PostgreSQL’s units, where 1 MB is 1,024 kB. InnoDB (MySQL 8.4) and SQLite appear where they report differently; no MySQL server was run for this article, so its names come from the manual and no InnoDB output is shown. The last sections use the signals together on four common incidents: commits that got slow, a restart that takes too long, a log directory filling the disk, and a table or undo history that keeps growing.</p>
    <p>Two neighbouring questions have their own articles. Why one query is slow, including lock waits and plans, belongs to <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/">Understanding database performance</a>. How large the cache should be, and what it keeps, belongs to <a href="/learn/databases/relational/buffer-pool/">The buffer pool</a>, the companion on the cache. Here the cache appears only as far as watching it needs.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="reporting"><h2 id="reporting">Where each engine reports</h2>
      <p>PostgreSQL keeps <a href={`${docs}monitoring-stats.html`}>cumulative statistics</a> in views whose names begin with <code>pg_stat_</code>, and writes events such as checkpoints and crash recovery to its server log. InnoDB reports through MySQL’s <a href={`${mysql}server-status-variables.html`}>global status variables</a>, read with <code>SHOW GLOBAL STATUS</code>; through a larger set of counters in <a href={`${mysql}innodb-information-schema-metrics-table.html`}><code>information_schema.INNODB_METRICS</code></a>, only some of which are switched on by default; and through a text report, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-standard-monitor.html`}><code>SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS</code></a>. Its events go to the MySQL error log. SQLite has no server, so nothing collects numbers on your behalf. The application asks: <a href={`${sqlite}pragma.html`}>PRAGMA</a> statements return the state of the log and its checkpoints, the C function <a href={`${sqlite}c3ref/c_dbstatus_options.html`}><code>sqlite3_db_status()</code></a> reports one connection’s cache hits and misses, and a callback registered for <a href={`${sqlite}c3ref/log.html`}><code>sqlite3_log()</code></a> receives notices such as a recovery. The sizes of the database’s files on disk are a signal too.</p>
      <p>Most of these numbers are counters that only grow: blocks read since the statistics were last reset, bytes of log written, checkpoints completed. One reading of a counter says little. Take two readings a known interval apart and divide the difference by the interval, and you have a rate: reads per second, log bytes per second. Monitoring systems do this for you, but you need to know when the counters restart. MySQL’s status variables start from zero when the server starts. PostgreSQL saves its statistics on a clean shutdown and, its documentation says, resets all of them after crash recovery. A rate computed across a restart means nothing. PostgreSQL also caches what a transaction has read from these views until the transaction ends, so a monitoring script that wraps several samples in one transaction sees the same numbers each time; take each sample in its own transaction, as psql does by default; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/#waiting">Understanding database performance</a> shows the same precaution for the activity view.</p>
      <p>Some of these signals exist only if they were set up before the incident. The incidents below assume this much:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>a monitoring role that can read other sessions: <code>pg_monitor</code> in PostgreSQL, the <code>PROCESS</code> privilege in MySQL for the InnoDB status report and transaction table;</li>
        <li>PostgreSQL’s <code>track_wal_io_timing</code> for log flush times and <code>track_io_timing</code> for data files, both off by default, and the <code>pg_buffercache</code> extension to count dirty pages;</li>
        <li>any <code>INNODB_METRICS</code> counter you rely on that is off by default, enabled with <code>innodb_monitor_enable</code>;</li>
        <li>a <code>sqlite3_log()</code> callback in each SQLite application, without which a recovery leaves no trace.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Few of these signals have a correct value. A checkpoint every five minutes is ordinary on one server and a warning on another. What helps is knowing your own server’s readings on an ordinary day, so that a change stands out. Each incident at the end starts from such a change.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="cache"><h2 id="cache">Reads and the cache</h2>
      <p>Every page the engine needs is either already in the buffer pool, a <strong>hit</strong>, or has to be fetched, a <strong>read</strong>. Hits divided by hits plus reads, over an interval, is the <strong>hit ratio</strong>: the share of page requests answered from memory.</p>
      <p>In PostgreSQL, <code>blks_hit</code> and <code>blks_read</code> in <code>pg_stat_database</code> count them per database, and <code>pg_stat_io</code> splits them by the kind of process that asked. A PostgreSQL read is a request to the operating system, which may answer from its own file cache, so the documentation is careful to say these counters don’t distinguish data fetched from disk from data already in the kernel’s cache. InnoDB’s pair is <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_read_requests</code>, every logical page request, and <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_reads</code>, requests the pool could not satisfy and had to read from disk. Because InnoDB bypasses the operating system’s cache for its data files by default on Linux, its reads are closer to real drive reads. SQLite counts per connection, as <code>SQLITE_DBSTATUS_CACHE_HIT</code> and <code>SQLITE_DBSTATUS_CACHE_MISS</code>.</p>
      <p>Sample both counters, with the commit count (<code>xact_commit</code> in <code>pg_stat_database</code>, MySQL’s <code>Com_commit</code>), at the start and end of an interval, and you have reads per second and reads per transaction as well as the ratio. In the local run, the sessions serving pgbench made 1,048,459 hits and 40,196 reads in two minutes: 96%, or about 335 reads a second. What those numbers mean for the size of the pool, and why the ratio alone misleads, is the subject of <a href="/learn/databases/relational/buffer-pool/">The buffer pool</a>.</p>
      <p>One cache signal belongs with writing rather than reading. <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_wait_free</code> counts the times InnoDB needed a page slot, found no clean one, and waited while dirty pages were flushed. The manual says it should be small when the pool is sized well. A count that keeps rising means reads are waiting for writes, which is the subject of the next section.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="writers"><h2 id="writers">Dirty pages and who writes them</h2>
      <p>A dirty page has to be written to its file before its slot can hold a different page. How many pages are dirty matters less than which process ends up writing them, because only one of the possible writers makes a query wait.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL has three. The <strong>checkpointer</strong> writes every page dirtied before a checkpoint, spread over most of the interval to the next one. The <strong>background writer</strong> wakes every 200 ms (<code>bgwriter_delay</code>) and writes dirty pages when clean ones look scarce. Its <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-resource.html#RUNTIME-CONFIG-RESOURCE-BACKGROUND-WRITER`}>documentation</a> gives the reason: it makes it less likely that a process serving queries will find no clean buffer and have to write dirty ones itself. The third is the session’s own process. When it needs a slot and the page it would evict is dirty, it writes the page itself, and its query waits for that write. <code>pg_stat_io</code> counts each kind of writer separately.</p>
      <Sql code={writers} label="PostgreSQL: page writes by kind of process" />
      <p>The query returns totals since the counters were last reset, which on a production server may be months. Run it twice and subtract, or reset first with <code>pg_stat_reset_shared('io')</code>, as the local run did; the shares are what to compare.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Page writes by kind of process in the local run">
        <table>
          <caption>Observed in the local run: two minutes at 400 transactions a second, 30-second checkpoints, with the counters reset just before.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>backend_type</th><th>writes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>checkpointer</code></td><td>34,165</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>background writer</code></td><td>8,140</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>client backend</code></td><td>19</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>autovacuum worker</code></td><td>5</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>That is the healthy shape: the checkpointer and background writer do almost all the writing, and the sessions serving queries (<code>client backend</code>) almost none. The documentation names the other shape directly: large numbers of writes by client backends “could indicate a misconfiguration of shared buffers or of the checkpointer.” In practice it means pages are being dirtied faster than the background processes clean them, or the pool is too small for the pages in use, and some queries pay for a write before they can read. A partner signal is <code>maxwritten_clean</code> in <code>pg_stat_bgwriter</code>: the rounds in which the background writer stopped at its limit of 100 pages (<code>bgwriter_lru_maxpages</code>). It was 9 in the local run; a steady rise alongside backend writes says the limit is holding it back. The same view reports <code>evictions</code>, pages pushed out to make room. Evictions by client backends rise with reads, so they belong to the cache question in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/buffer-pool/">The buffer pool</a> rather than to writing.</p>
      <p>To count dirty pages directly, the <code>pg_buffercache</code> extension that ships with PostgreSQL provides <code>pg_buffercache_summary()</code>. In the local run the count climbed from almost nothing to 14,921 of 16,384 buffers in the first half-minute, then settled between about 10,000 and 12,000 once checkpoints were writing. A high count on its own is not a fault: most of the pool was dirty here while the sessions wrote almost nothing themselves. It becomes one when it stays high together with writes by client backends. Figure 1 below shows the whole run.</p>
      <p>InnoDB reports <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_pages_dirty</code> against <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_pages_total</code>; <code>SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS</code> calls the same count “Modified db pages”. Its writers are <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool-flushing.html`}>page cleaner threads</a>, which write from two ends: the oldest changes first, which advances the checkpoint, and pages near the end of the eviction list, which keeps clean pages available. When neither keeps up, a session waits for a free page, and <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_wait_free</code> rises. Adaptive flushing raises the page cleaners’ rate as the dirty share passes <code>innodb_max_dirty_pages_pct_lwm</code> (10%) and as the redo log fills; at <code>innodb_max_dirty_pages_pct</code>, 90 by default, InnoDB flushes aggressively. <code>SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS</code> shows the redo side in its LOG section, as “Log sequence number” and “Last checkpoint at”.</p>
      <Sql code={innodbPool} label="InnoDB: cache and dirty-page counters" />
      <p>SQLite has no background writer. A transaction’s changed pages stay in the connection’s cache and are written at commit, to the WAL file in WAL mode. If the cache fills before the commit, SQLite has to write some pages early, and <code>SQLITE_DBSTATUS_CACHE_SPILL</code> counts those; its documentation suggests a larger cache when it rises.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="checkpoints"><h2 id="checkpoints">Reading checkpoints</h2>
      <p>A checkpoint writes the pages dirtied before a log position, then records that position as the place crash recovery will start. Three things about checkpoints are worth watching: how often they happen, what started them, and how long their writing took. PostgreSQL reports all three in its server log, one pair of lines per checkpoint, because <code>log_checkpoints</code> is on by default.</p>

      <h3 id="checkpoint-line">The checkpoint log line</h3>
      <p>Here is one pair from the local run, wrapped to fit. Only the time is kept from each line’s prefix. For the experiment, <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> was lowered from its default of 5 minutes to its minimum, 30 seconds, so that several cycles fit in two minutes.</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="A checkpoint in the PostgreSQL server log"><pre><code>{checkpointLog}</code></pre></div>
      <p>Read it in this order.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>What started it.</strong> The word after <code>checkpoint starting:</code> is the cause. <code>time</code> means <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> passed. <code>wal</code> means the log written since the last checkpoint reached the limit set by <code>max_wal_size</code>. <code>immediate force wait</code> appears for a <code>CHECKPOINT</code> command, and <code>end-of-recovery</code> for the one that finishes crash recovery.</li>
        <li><strong>How much it wrote.</strong> 8,348 buffers, 51% of shared buffers.</li>
        <li><strong>How long the writing took.</strong> <code>write=27.013 s</code> looks alarming and isn’t. PostgreSQL spreads the writes over about 90% of the interval (<code>checkpoint_completion_target</code> is 0.9), and 90% of 30 seconds is 27. A long write phase is the design working. <code>sync=0.054 s</code> is the time spent waiting for the data files to be flushed at the end, and that is the number that grows when storage is slow or overloaded. <code>longest</code> names the slowest single file.</li>
        <li><strong>How much log it covered.</strong> <code>distance=115037 kB</code> is the log written between this checkpoint’s start and the previous one’s: about 112 MB in 30 seconds here. It is a log rate you get for free. It is not quite what a crash would replay: replay starts from the last <em>completed</em> checkpoint, so a crash while the next one is writing replays nearly two intervals’ worth.</li>
        <li><strong>What happened to old log files.</strong> <code>7 recycled</code> means seven 16 MB segments behind the previous checkpoint were renamed for reuse. If files are kept instead, the section on log retention below explains why.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="cycle">A checkpoint cycle, recorded</h3>
      <p>Figure 1 plots the same run sampled once a second: how fast the log was written, and how many buffers were dirty. The shaded bands are the three complete checkpoints, from their start line to their completion line.</p>
      <figure class="ws-figure" aria-labelledby="ws-cycle-title">
