            <path class="ws-dirty" d={dirtyPath} />
            {ticks.map(t => <text class="ws-tick" x={x(t)} y="272" text-anchor="middle">{t}</text>)}
            <text class="ws-tick" x="348" y="286" text-anchor="end">seconds</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Recorded on a laptop: PostgreSQL 18.6, pgbench at 400 transactions a second, 128 MB of shared buffers (16,384 pages), checkpoints every 30 seconds. A manual checkpoint ran just before the first sample, which is why the log rate also starts high. The rates belong to this run, not to PostgreSQL in general.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>Two patterns repeat. The dirty-page count rises until a checkpoint starts writing, then holds steady, because the checkpointer and the background writer now clean pages about as fast as the workload dirties them. And the log rate jumps at every checkpoint start, from under 3 MB a second to over 5, then decays over the next 20 seconds, with no change in the workload.</p>
      <p>The jump is full-page images. After a checkpoint starts, the first change to each page writes a copy of the whole page into the log, so a crash can repair a torn page (<a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#torn-pages">Storage and durability</a> explains why). Right after a checkpoint, almost every change is a first change; as the interval goes on, more changes land on pages already copied. <code>pg_stat_wal</code> counts the images in <code>wal_fpi</code>. Over the two minutes it recorded 55,653 images among 329,288 log records. A separate 20-second run straight after a checkpoint, measured with <code>pg_waldump --stats</code>, found full-page images made up 95% of the log’s bytes. pgbench updates rows scattered over a table of 33,000 pages, so most changes land on a page not yet copied. A workload that keeps changing the same few pages would show a much smaller share.</p>
      <p>A periodic rise in log volume and in write I/O, lined up with checkpoint starts, is therefore normal. What deserves attention is a rise in commit latency at the same moments, which the incidents section returns to.</p>

      <h3 id="timed-requested">Timed or requested</h3>
      <p><code>pg_stat_checkpointer</code>, which in PostgreSQL 17 and 18 holds the checkpoint counters that used to sit in <code>pg_stat_bgwriter</code>, keeps the same story as counters: <code>num_timed</code> for checkpoints started by the timer, <code>num_requested</code> for those requested by the log size limit, a <code>CHECKPOINT</code> command or a backup, and <code>num_done</code> for those that actually ran. It also sums <code>write_time</code>, <code>sync_time</code> and <code>buffers_written</code>.</p>
      <p>On a server with steady load, most checkpoints should be timed. A run of requested ones means the log is reaching <code>max_wal_size</code> before the timer fires, and PostgreSQL says so in the log whenever two checkpoints caused by the log filling come closer together than <code>checkpoint_warning</code>, 30 seconds by default:</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The PostgreSQL warning for frequent checkpoints"><pre><code>{tooOften}</code></pre></div>
      <p>That warning came from repeating the same two-minute workload with <code>max_wal_size</code> lowered from its default of 1 GB to 64 MB.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The same workload with two values of max_wal_size">
        <table>
          <caption>The same two-minute workload, run once with each setting, on one laptop. The counts are of checkpoints started; <code>num_done</code>, which counts completed ones, was 3 and 12, because the last checkpoint of each run was still writing.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>Reading</th><th><code>max_wal_size = 1GB</code></th><th><code>max_wal_size = 64MB</code></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Checkpoints timed / requested</td><td>4 / 0</td><td>0 / 13</td></tr>
            <tr><td>“Too frequently” warnings</td><td>0</td><td>13</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pages written by checkpointer + background writer</td><td>34,165 + 8,140</td><td>47,262 + 33</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Log written (<code>wal_bytes</code>)</td><td>442 MB</td><td>421 MB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Full-page images (<code>wal_fpi</code>)</td><td>55,653</td><td>52,475</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Average transaction latency (pgbench)</td><td>1.90 ms</td><td>2.62 ms</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The signals behave as the documentation predicts: every checkpoint became a requested one, each followed the last by 6 to 10 seconds, and the log said so 13 times. Average transaction latency rose from 1.90 ms to 2.62 ms, and that is the cost users would notice. pgbench’s figure includes time a transaction waited for its scheduled start, which also rose, so not all of the difference is slower commits; with one run of each on a laptop, take the rise as an indication, not a measurement. The other costs were smaller than you might expect. Page writes rose by about 12%, and the log hardly changed, for the reason in the previous section: in this workload nearly every change hit a page not yet copied since the last checkpoint, at either interval. Where the same pages change many times a minute, frequent checkpoints multiply full-page images instead. So treat the warning as a reason to look at commit latency, log volume and write I/O, and raise <code>max_wal_size</code>, which is the documentation’s <a href={`${docs}wal-configuration.html`}>first response</a>, rather than as proof of a particular cost.</p>

      <h3 id="innodb-checkpoint">InnoDB: how full the redo log is</h3>
      <p>InnoDB has no checkpoint events to log, because its checkpoint moves continuously behind the oldest page still dirty. What you watch instead is the distance between the newest log record and the checkpoint, often called the <strong>checkpoint age</strong>, against the redo log’s fixed size. <code>Innodb_redo_log_current_lsn</code> minus <code>Innodb_redo_log_checkpoint_lsn</code> gives it in bytes, and <code>Innodb_redo_log_logical_size</code> reports the span of redo still in use directly. The size it is compared with is <code>innodb_redo_log_capacity</code>, 100 MB by default in MySQL 8.4.</p>
      <Sql code={innodbRedo} label="InnoDB: redo log positions and capacity" />
      <p>Normally the age rises and falls well below capacity as the page cleaners keep pace. When it approaches capacity, the manual describes InnoDB flushing dirty pages more aggressively, with a temporary drop in throughput if the log does fill; in the worst case the error log reports <a href="https://github.com/mysql/mysql-server/blob/8.4/share/messages_to_error_log.txt">“Redo log is running out of free space, pausing user threads”</a> and suggests a larger <code>innodb_redo_log_capacity</code>. So a sawtooth of throughput, with the checkpoint age pinned near the limit, is InnoDB’s version of PostgreSQL’s too-frequent checkpoints: the log is too small for the write rate, or the page cleaners are held back by <code>innodb_io_capacity</code> (10,000 by default in 8.4) or by slow storage.</p>

      <h3 id="sqlite-checkpoint">SQLite: what a checkpoint returns</h3>
      <p>In WAL mode, SQLite <a href={`${sqlite}wal.html#automatic_checkpoint`}>checkpoints automatically</a> when a commit leaves the WAL file at 1,000 pages or more, copying the logged pages back into the database file. <code>PRAGMA wal_checkpoint</code> runs one by hand and returns three numbers: whether a blocking checkpoint was stopped by another connection (1) or not (0), the number of pages in the WAL file, and the number of those pages copied back. Run with no other connection open, the result below shows a checkpoint that finished: everything in the log was copied.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="A completed SQLite checkpoint">
        <table>
          <caption>Observed with SQLite 3.54.0 after 5,000 small inserts in WAL mode.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>busy</th><th>log</th><th>checkpointed</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td><code>PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(PASSIVE)</code></td><td>0</td><td>413</td><td>413</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>When the third number stays below the second, something is stopping the copy. The section on old versions shows what.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="log"><h2 id="log">How fast the log grows, and why it stays</h2>
      <p>The log is written at the rate of change, and kept until nothing needs it. Both halves show up in production: the rate sets the disk, replication and archive bandwidth you need, and anything that stops old log being released turns into a disk that fills.</p>

      <h3 id="log-rate">Measuring the rate</h3>
      <p>PostgreSQL gives the rate three ways. <code>pg_stat_wal</code> counts records, full-page images and bytes. The current log position, <code>pg_current_wal_lsn()</code>, is itself a byte offset, so the difference between two readings, from <code>pg_wal_lsn_diff()</code>, is the bytes written in between. And each checkpoint’s <code>distance</code> gives the log per checkpoint interval.</p>
      <Sql code={rates} label="PostgreSQL: sample the log position and log counters" />
      <p>In the local run the log grew by 442 MB in two minutes at 400 transactions a second: about 3.7 MB a second, or roughly 9.5 kB per transaction of five short statements, most of it full-page images. That is the kind of figure to know for your own server, because it sets how fast an archive or a replica has to keep up. Its share of full-page images tells you whether checkpoint spacing is part of the cost. PostgreSQL can compress the images with <code>wal_compression</code>, off by default. More indexes on a table also mean more log per row changed, since every index entry that changes is logged too.</p>
      <p>InnoDB reports bytes written to the redo log as <code>Innodb_os_log_written</code>, and its current position as <code>Innodb_redo_log_current_lsn</code>; sample either twice. SQLite’s <code>SQLITE_DBSTATUS_CACHE_WRITE</code> counts the pages a connection has written to the WAL file.</p>

      <h3 id="retention">What holds the log back</h3>
      <p>PostgreSQL removes or recycles a log segment at a checkpoint, and only once nothing still needs it. Recovery needs everything from the latest checkpoint’s start. With archiving on, a segment must have been archived. A <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/replication/#retention">replication slot</a> holds everything its consumer has not yet confirmed. And <code>wal_keep_size</code>, 0 by default, keeps a fixed amount for standbys. <code>max_wal_size</code> is not a limit on any of these. The <a href={`${docs}wal-configuration.html`}>documentation</a> calls it “never a hard limit” and says that if archiving fails repeatedly, “old WAL files will accumulate in <code>pg_wal</code> until the situation is resolved,” and that a slow or failed standby using a slot has the same effect.</p>
      <p>To see that happen, the local server was restarted with archiving on and an archive command that always fails, <code>false</code>, with <code>max_wal_size</code> at 256 MB, then given 25 seconds of unthrottled pgbench. The log said this once a second:</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="A failing archive command in the PostgreSQL server log"><pre><code>{archiveLog}</code></pre></div>
      <p>These queries show the effect from inside the database:</p>
      <Sql code={retention} label="PostgreSQL: archive state, slots and the size of pg_wal" />
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="pg_wal while archiving fails and after it is repaired">
        <table>
          <caption>Observed in the local run. <code>max_wal_size</code> was 256 MB throughout; the starting size is segments left for reuse by earlier runs with a 1 GB limit.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>Moment</th><th><code>pg_wal</code> size</th><th>Segments waiting to archive</th><th><code>failed_count</code></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Before the workload</td><td>576 MB</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>After 25 s of writes, archiving failing</td><td>2,080 MB</td><td>129</td><td>37</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Archive command repaired, archive caught up</td><td>2,096 MB</td><td>0</td><td>42</td></tr>
            <tr><td>After the next checkpoint</td><td>256 MB</td><td>0</td><td>42</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Two things in that table are easy to miss. The log grew to eight times <code>max_wal_size</code> without any error. The checkpoint lines showed it instead: every one read <code>0 removed, 0 recycled</code>, some with files added, and the server warned that checkpoints were occurring too frequently. A run of checkpoints that recycle nothing is the retention signal to learn. And repairing the archive did not free any space by itself: the backlog was archived within 20 seconds, but the files stayed until the next checkpoint removed them. On a real incident that can be minutes later. The count of waiting segments is the number of files in <code>pg_wal/archive_status</code> ending <code>.ready</code>, which <code>pg_ls_archive_statusdir()</code> lists.</p>
      <p>A replication slot whose consumer has gone looks similar. A slot created and left unused during 15 more seconds of the same workload held back 706 MB, and <code>pg_replication_slots</code> showed it as <code>active = f</code> with <code>wal_status</code> <code>extended</code>, meaning the retained log has gone past <code>max_wal_size</code>. <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> caps what a slot may hold; it is unlimited by default. Setting it to 512 MB and running a checkpoint freed the space and marked the slot <code>lost</code>, with <code>invalidation_reason</code> <code>wal_removed</code>. The consumer would then need a fresh copy of the database, which is the trade that setting makes; the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/replication/#retention">replication article</a> discusses it.</p>
      <p>A long transaction does not by itself keep PostgreSQL’s log. It keeps old row versions, the subject of the next section. Through a logical replication slot it can hold the log as well, because decoding has to be able to read that transaction from its start.</p>

      <h3 id="log-other-engines">In InnoDB and SQLite</h3>
      <p>InnoDB’s <a href={`${mysql}innodb-redo-log.html`}>redo log</a> cannot fill a disk: it is a set of files in the <code>#innodb_redo</code> directory whose total size is fixed by <code>innodb_redo_log_capacity</code>. Pressure on it shows as the flushing stalls described above. What grows in MySQL is the <a href={`${mysql}binary-log.html`}>binary log</a>, kept for replicas and point-in-time recovery. Its files are removed when they are older than <code>binlog_expire_logs_seconds</code>, 30 days by default, or by <code>PURGE BINARY LOGS</code>. MySQL does not keep them for a disconnected replica: <a href={`${mysql}purge-binary-logs.html`}><code>PURGE BINARY LOGS</code></a> won’t delete a file a connected replica is reading, but a replica that is offline when its files expire cannot continue. A binary log directory that grows faster than expected is a write-volume question, and one that never shrinks is an expiry setting. InnoDB’s undo, which a long transaction does make grow, is in the next section.</p>
      <p>SQLite’s WAL file grows when checkpoints cannot finish, because a reader is still using older frames, or when automatic checkpoints have been switched off. It does not shrink after a checkpoint either: SQLite starts writing at the beginning of the file again and keeps its size, unless <code>PRAGMA journal_size_limit</code> is set or a checkpoint is run in <code>TRUNCATE</code> mode. A large <code>-wal</code> file next to a quiet database is not necessarily a problem; one that keeps growing is.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="flush"><h2 id="flush">Flush time and commit latency</h2>
      <p>A commit with the default settings waits for the log to be flushed to storage, so the time a flush takes is the least a commit can take. Group commit lets one flush serve several commits at once, so throughput can grow with concurrency while each commit still waits at least one flush. When commit latency rises and nothing else has changed, the flush is the first thing to measure.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL 18 reports log I/O in <code>pg_stat_io</code>, in the rows whose <code>object</code> is <code>wal</code>: <code>writes</code> and <code>fsyncs</code>, and with <code>track_wal_io_timing</code> on, which it is not by default, <code>write_time</code> and <code>fsync_time</code>. In PostgreSQL 17 these were columns of <code>pg_stat_wal</code>; in 18 they moved here. Which column carries the flush depends on <code>wal_sync_method</code>. With <code>fdatasync</code>, the Linux default, every flush is a separate call, counted in <code>fsyncs</code> and timed in <code>fsync_time</code>. With an <code>open_datasync</code> or <code>open_sync</code> method, each write is itself synchronous, so the flush time is inside <code>write_time</code> and <code>fsyncs</code> stays at zero. The laptop used <code>open_datasync</code>, the default on macOS, and showed exactly that: 47,076 log writes by the sessions themselves, taking 5.7 seconds in total, about 0.12 ms each, and no fsyncs. Don’t take 0.12 ms as the cost of a durable flush. macOS acknowledges these writes before the drive has made them durable, which is the early acknowledgement <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#flush">Storage and durability</a> describes and the warning at the end of this section is about. That article measured about 0.04 ms a commit with the same setting on the same laptop; the difference here is several sessions sharing the log, not the device.</p>
      <p>Two more PostgreSQL signals point at the same place. While a session waits for its flush, <code>pg_stat_activity</code> shows the IO wait event <code>WalSync</code> (or <code>WalWrite</code> with an <code>open_*</code> method). Because of group commit, most other committing sessions are queued behind that one, and they show the LWLock wait <code>WALWrite</code>, “waiting for WAL buffers to be written to disk”. It looks like a lock wait but is the same storage cost. Sessions waiting in IPC <code>SyncRep</code> are waiting for a synchronous standby to confirm, which is a replication cost, not a local flush. And <code>wal_buffers_full</code> in <code>pg_stat_wal</code> counts times the in-memory log buffer filled and had to be written early; it was 0 throughout the local run, and a steady rise suggests a larger <code>wal_buffers</code>.</p>
      <p>InnoDB counts redo flushes in <code>Innodb_os_log_fsyncs</code> and waits for a full log buffer in <code>Innodb_log_waits</code>. Timings come from the Performance Schema, whose file I/O summary tables report time spent on the redo log’s files under the event <code>wait/io/file/innodb/innodb_log_file</code>. SQLite has nothing inside to report; time the commits in the application.</p>
      <p>Wherever the numbers come from, compare them with a baseline for the storage itself. PostgreSQL ships <code>pg_test_fsync</code>, which times each sync method on a given directory, and the operating system’s own disk statistics show the device’s queue and wait times. If a commit takes 5 ms and a flush on that volume takes 4 ms, the commit is not slow; the storage is. That baseline also catches the opposite warning from <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#flush">Storage and durability</a>: a flush far faster than the device could possibly manage suggests a cache is answering it.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="history"><h2 id="history">Old row versions that pile up</h2>
      <p>Every engine keeps old versions of changed rows until no reader can need them, and in every engine one long-running transaction or snapshot stops the cleanup. What grows depends on where the old versions live, so the signal differs.</p>
      <p>In PostgreSQL, old versions stay in the table as dead rows until VACUUM removes them, and it can remove only those older than the oldest snapshot still in use. Two queries cover the usual investigation: which sessions hold the oldest snapshots, and which tables have the most dead rows and when autovacuum last ran on them.</p>
      <Sql code={pgVersions} label="PostgreSQL: the oldest snapshots, and dead rows per table" />
      <p>A session at the top of the first list with an old <code>xact_start</code>, often <code>idle in transaction</code>, together with tables whose <code>n_dead_tup</code> keeps rising while <code>last_autovacuum</code> is recent, means vacuum is running but cannot remove what it finds. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/#maintenance">Row versions, transactions, and vacuum</a> works through that diagnosis, and the case where vacuum simply can’t keep up.</p>
      <p>In InnoDB, old versions are undo records, and the number of transactions’ worth of undo waiting for purge is the <a href={`${mysql}innodb-purge-configuration.html`}><strong>history list length</strong></a>. It appears as “History list length” in the TRANSACTIONS part of <code>SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS</code> and as the counter <code>trx_rseg_history_len</code>, which is enabled by default. There is no status variable for it. On a healthy server it rises and falls as purge keeps up. A history list that only climbs means purge is blocked, and the usual cause is the oldest open transaction, which <code>INNODB_TRX</code> lists with its start time and thread.</p>
      <Sql code={innodbHistory} label="InnoDB: history list length and the oldest transactions" />
      <p>The cost shows up in two places. Readers that need old versions rebuild them from longer undo chains, so queries slow down. And the undo tablespace files, <code>undo_001</code> and <code>undo_002</code> by default, grow; with <code>innodb_undo_log_truncate</code>, on by default, InnoDB truncates one once it passes <code>innodb_max_undo_log_size</code> (1 GB) and purge has freed it. <code>innodb_max_purge_lag</code>, 0 by default, can delay writes when the history list passes a limit, which trades write latency for a bounded history.</p>
      <p>SQLite keeps old versions only in WAL mode, as frames in the WAL file that an open reader may still need. A checkpoint cannot copy frames past the snapshot of the oldest reader, and cannot restart the log while any reader is using it; SQLite’s documentation calls the result <a href={`${sqlite}wal.html#avoiding_excessively_large_wal_files`}>checkpoint starvation</a>. This run opened a reading transaction on a second connection, then kept writing:</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="SQLite WAL growth with a reader held open">
        <table>
          <caption>Observed with SQLite 3.54.0; each step is 5,000 small inserts. Automatic checkpoints at 1,000 pages were left on.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>State</th><th><code>-wal</code> file</th><th><code>wal_checkpoint(PASSIVE)</code></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>No reader</td><td>1.6 MB</td><td>0, 413, 413</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reader open, first step</td><td>1.6 MB</td><td>0, 412, 0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reader open, second step</td><td>3.2 MB</td><td>0, 825, 0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reader open, third step</td><td>4.9 MB</td><td>0, 1248, 0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reader closed</td><td>4.9 MB</td><td>0, 1248, 1248</td></tr>
            <tr><td>More writes, then <code>wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE)</code></td><td>0</td><td>0, 0, 0</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>With the reader open, the log passed the 1,000-page automatic checkpoint and nothing was copied back. A <code>TRUNCATE</code> checkpoint tried at that point returned 1 in its first column: blocked. Once the reader finished, an ordinary checkpoint copied everything, but the file kept its size, and new writes reused it, until a later <code>TRUNCATE</code> checkpoint. The signal to watch, then, is a WAL file that keeps growing together with a <code>checkpointed</code> count that stays behind <code>log</code>; the cause is a connection holding a read transaction open.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="restart"><h2 id="restart">Reading a restart</h2>
      <p>After a crash, the server’s log is the record of recovery: where replay began, how much log it read, whether it reached a clean end, and when the database opened. To get a real example, the local server was stopped with <code>pg_ctl stop -m immediate</code>, which ends every process at once, without a shutdown checkpoint, while pgbench was running flat out. The startup log follows, with dates and process IDs removed and two lines shortened.</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="PostgreSQL crash recovery in the server log"><pre><code>{restartLog}</code></pre></div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>“database system was interrupted; last known up at 17:31:27”</strong> gives the last time the control file was updated, normally at the last completed checkpoint. It is not the time of the crash, which was 17:32:07. A checkpoint had started at 17:32:06 but not finished, so it doesn’t count.</li>
        <li><strong>“not properly shut down; automatic recovery in progress”</strong> confirms this is crash recovery, not a clean start.</li>
        <li><strong>“redo starts at 0/47181CB8”</strong> is the start of that last completed checkpoint. <strong>“redo done at 0/6A1D6808”</strong> is the last record replayed. The two positions are byte offsets, so recovery replayed 560 MB of log, in 2.8 seconds on this laptop.</li>
        <li><strong>“invalid record length … expected at least 24, got 0”</strong> is how replay finds the end of the log: the next record is empty. Seen once, just before “redo done”, it is normal. The message depends on what follows the last record. Here the log ended in a segment file that had not been used before, so the next bytes were zeros. If the next page belongs to a segment file recycled from an earlier cycle, the same end is reported as “unexpected pageaddr”, because that page still carries its old address. Another crash test on the same laptop ended that way. A torn final write can show as an incorrect checksum or a bad prev-link instead. The manual doesn’t list these; Tom Lane described “unexpected pageaddr” on the PostgreSQL mailing list as <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/message-id/5031.1296689542%40sss.pgh.pa.us">one of the standard tests for detecting end of WAL</a>. Each is normal only at the true end of the log. What deserves investigation is replay ending well short of where the log stood at the crash, or an error that stops startup.</li>
        <li><strong>“checkpoint starting: end-of-recovery”</strong> writes out everything replay changed, here 16,362 buffers, nearly the whole pool, before the database opens.</li>
        <li><strong>“ready to accept connections”</strong> ends the outage. From process start to this line took 3 seconds.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The amount of log to replay is what decides the time. It is roughly the log written since the last checkpoint began, so a crash late in a long checkpoint interval replays the most, and <code>max_wal_size</code> and <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> together bound it. PostgreSQL starts a checkpoint for log size once about <code>max_wal_size</code> / (1 + <code>checkpoint_completion_target</code>) has been written, a rule that lives in the source (<a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres/blob/REL_18_STABLE/src/backend/access/transam/xlog.c"><code>CalculateCheckpointSegments</code> in xlog.c</a>) rather than the manual, here about 540 MB, so that the log since the previous checkpoint stays under the limit while the new one writes. One had just started at the crash, so replay covered 560 MB. That rule also gives the worst case: close to <code>max_wal_size</code>. Replay speed depends on storage and on how many replayed pages must first be read, so measure it on a copy of your own server rather than scaling from these numbers. A long replay is not silent: every 10 seconds (<code>log_startup_progress_interval</code>) PostgreSQL logs a line of the form “redo in progress, elapsed time: …, current LSN: …”, and comparing that position with the end you expect tells you how far it has got. Remember also that the restart has reset the statistics counters.</p>
      <p>InnoDB’s error log tells the same story in its own words. The messages defined in the 8.4 server’s <a href="https://github.com/mysql/mysql-server/blob/8.4/share/messages_to_error_log.txt">error-log message file</a> include “Database was not shutdown normally!”, “Starting crash recovery.”, “Doing recovery: scanned up to log sequence number …”, “Applying a batch of … redo log records …” and “Apply batch completed!”. Then comes InnoDB’s second phase: transactions that were open at the crash are rolled back from undo, in the background, after the server has started accepting connections. A long one reports progress as “Still rolling back transaction …; … undo records rolled back out of … total”, and the manual warns that this rollback <a href={`${mysql}innodb-recovery.html`}>can take three or four times</a> as long as the transaction had been running. Restarting the server doesn’t cancel it; the documented escape is to start with <code>innodb_force_recovery</code> set to 3, which skips the rollback and is meant only for getting data out of a damaged server. So a MySQL server can be open and still slow, with new sessions meeting locks held by transactions being rolled back.</p>
      <p>SQLite recovers on the first open after a crash. In WAL mode that connection rebuilds the WAL index from the WAL file while holding an exclusive lock, so other connections get <code>SQLITE_BUSY</code> until it finishes; in rollback-journal mode it rolls back a hot journal. Neither appears anywhere unless the application has registered a <code>sqlite3_log()</code> callback, which then receives <code>SQLITE_NOTICE_RECOVER_WAL</code> or <code>SQLITE_NOTICE_RECOVER_ROLLBACK</code>. Registering one is the only way to know a recovery happened.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="incidents"><h2 id="incidents">Four incidents</h2>
      <p>Each incident below starts from what someone notices and works towards a mechanism, using the signals above. The order of the checks matters more than any one number: each step rules out a whole family of causes before the next.</p>

      <h3 id="slow-commits">Commits got slow</h3>
      <p>Requests that write are slower, and the database time is in <code>COMMIT</code> or in single-statement writes. First, confirm the time is spent waiting on storage and not on locks: sample <code>pg_stat_activity</code> during the slowdown. Sessions in <code>WalSync</code>, <code>WalWrite</code> or the LWLock <code>WALWrite</code> are waiting for the log, and sessions in <code>SyncRep</code> for a synchronous standby, a question for <a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-failover/">Replicas and failover</a>; sessions in other lock waits belong to <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/#waiting">Understanding database performance</a>.</p>
      <p>If they are waiting for the log, compare the flush time per call (<code>fsync_time</code> or <code>write_time</code> over the count, for the <code>wal</code> rows in <code>pg_stat_io</code>) with your baseline. Those times exist only if <code>track_wal_io_timing</code> was already on; without it, you have the wait events and the operating system’s disk statistics. A flush that got slower with an unchanged workload points at the storage: another tenant on a shared volume, a cloud volume out of burst credits, a failing device. Check the operating system’s disk statistics for the same window. If flush time is unchanged but commits per second rose, the log is simply busier; group commit absorbs some of that.</p>
      <p>If the slowdown comes and goes, line it up with the checkpoint log. Slow commits during the first part of each checkpoint, with a higher log rate and many full-page images, mean checkpoint writes and image-heavy log are competing with commit flushes on the same storage. Longer, timed checkpoints and a larger <code>max_wal_size</code> spread that cost. A high <code>sync=</code> time in the checkpoint lines tells you the data-file flushes themselves are slow. And if client backends show page writes in <code>pg_stat_io</code>, queries are cleaning pages that the background writer should have.</p>
      <p>On InnoDB, check whether the checkpoint age sits near <code>innodb_redo_log_capacity</code>, whether <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_wait_free</code> is rising, and the timing of the redo log’s file I/O in the Performance Schema. On SQLite, which allows one writer at a time, slow commits are as often a long write transaction holding the lock as a slow flush; time both, and count the <code>SQLITE_BUSY</code> results and <code>busy_timeout</code> waits that show the lock.</p>

      <h3 id="long-restart">A restart that takes too long</h3>
      <p>The server crashed or was killed, and it is still not accepting connections. Read the startup log first. “Redo starts at” gives the starting position and the progress lines give the current one. The end is roughly where the log stood at the crash: the log written since the last <em>completed</em> checkpoint began, which you can estimate from the log rate and the time of that checkpoint’s “checkpoint starting” line, and which is at most about <code>max_wal_size</code>. If the progress position is advancing, recovery is working; the question is only time. If the replay finished but the server still isn’t ready, look at what came after it, such as the end-of-recovery checkpoint on slow storage.</p>
      <p>The lasting fix is to decide how long recovery may take and set checkpoints to match. Recovery time grows with the log since the last checkpoint, so the timer and <code>max_wal_size</code> are the controls, at the price of more frequent checkpoints. Measure replay speed on a copy before choosing, and don’t forget a cold cache afterwards: the server opens with the pages recovery touched, not the ones the workload needs.</p>
      <p>On InnoDB, also check the error log for rollback progress. A server that is open but slow after a crash may be rolling back a large transaction, and the fix is in the application: don’t run one transaction that changes millions of rows. On SQLite, a long first open after a crash is the WAL recovery, and the WAL file’s size tells you how much it has to read.</p>

      <h3 id="log-filling">The log directory is filling the disk</h3>
      <p>Disk alerts point at <code>pg_wal</code>, the binary log directory or a <code>-wal</code> file. In PostgreSQL, ask the three questions in order. Is archiving failing? <code>pg_stat_archiver</code> shows a rising <code>failed_count</code> and a recent <code>last_failed_time</code>, and the server log shows the archive command’s error. Is a slot holding the log? <code>pg_replication_slots</code> shows an inactive slot, or one far behind, with <code>wal_status</code> <code>extended</code>. Is <code>wal_keep_size</code> large? If none of these, the log is being written faster than checkpoints can retire it, which the checkpoint lines and the log rate will show.</p>
      <p>Fix the cause, not the files. Never delete segments from <code>pg_wal</code> by hand: recovery and replicas may need them, and a missing one can leave the server unable to start. Repair the archive destination or command, or decide that a dead slot’s consumer will be rebuilt and drop the slot. Then expect the space back at the next checkpoint, not at once; running <code>CHECKPOINT</code> brings it forward. To keep it from recurring, alert on archive failures and on slots that are inactive or behind, and consider <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> if losing a replica is better than losing the primary’s disk.</p>
      <p>In MySQL, a growing binary log directory means writes are heavy or expiry is too long for the disk; check <code>binlog_expire_logs_seconds</code> against how far your replicas can lag before purging. A SQLite <code>-wal</code> file that keeps growing means a reader that never finishes, or checkpoints switched off, as the history section showed.</p>

      <h3 id="history-growing">A table or undo history that keeps growing</h3>
      <p>A PostgreSQL table grows although its row count is steady, or InnoDB’s history list length climbs for hours. Both are old versions that cannot be cleaned up, and the first question is the same in both engines: what is the oldest transaction or snapshot, and who owns it? In PostgreSQL that is the session with the oldest <code>backend_xmin</code>, or a replication slot (the <code>xmin</code> and <code>catalog_xmin</code> columns of <code>pg_replication_slots</code>) or a prepared transaction (<code>pg_prepared_xacts</code>), neither of which appears in <code>pg_stat_activity</code>; in InnoDB the oldest row of <code>INNODB_TRX</code>. Usually it is a report or batch job, or a connection left idle inside a transaction by an application that forgot to commit.</p>
      <p>If no old transaction exists and the history still grows, cleanup is running but falling behind. In PostgreSQL, the autovacuum timestamps and the dead-row counts per table show whether a busy table is vacuumed often enough; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/#maintenance">Row versions, transactions, and vacuum</a> covers tuning it. In InnoDB, more purge threads (<code>innodb_purge_threads</code>) or a purge lag limit are the controls. A PostgreSQL table that grew will not shrink on its own when the backlog clears; the space is reused, and <code>VACUUM FULL</code> returns it by rewriting the table under an exclusive lock, a trade the row-versions article weighs. InnoDB’s undo files are truncated automatically once they pass <code>innodb_max_undo_log_size</code> and purge has caught up.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="summary"><h2 id="summary">What to watch, by engine</h2>
      <p>The same signals, gathered for reference. “Sample” means a counter to read twice and turn into a rate.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Storage signals in PostgreSQL, InnoDB and SQLite">
        <table class="ws-summary">
          <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>PostgreSQL 18</th><th>InnoDB (MySQL 8.4)</th><th>SQLite</th><th>A bad reading suggests</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Page reads</td><td>Sample <code>blks_read</code>, <code>blks_hit</code> (<code>pg_stat_database</code>); <code>reads</code>, <code>hits</code> (<code>pg_stat_io</code>)</td><td>Sample <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_reads</code>, <code>_read_requests</code></td><td><code>SQLITE_DBSTATUS_CACHE_MISS</code>, <code>_HIT</code></td><td>Reads per transaction rising: the pages in use no longer fit, or a scan is pushing them out</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dirty pages</td><td><code>pg_buffercache_summary()</code></td><td><code>Innodb_buffer_pool_pages_dirty</code> / <code>_pages_total</code></td><td>Not reported; <code>SQLITE_DBSTATUS_CACHE_SPILL</code> counts pages written early</td><td>High together with backend writes or <code>wait_free</code>: writers not keeping up</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Who writes pages</td><td>Sample <code>writes</code> by <code>backend_type</code> (<code>pg_stat_io</code>)</td><td>Sample <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_wait_free</code></td><td>Not applicable</td><td>Query sessions writing or waiting for free pages</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Checkpoints</td><td>Server log with <code>log_checkpoints</code>; <code>num_timed</code>, <code>num_requested</code> (<code>pg_stat_checkpointer</code>)</td><td>Checkpoint age: <code>Innodb_redo_log_current_lsn</code> − <code>_checkpoint_lsn</code> against <code>innodb_redo_log_capacity</code></td><td><code>PRAGMA wal_checkpoint</code> result</td><td>Mostly requested, “too frequently” warnings, age near capacity, or checkpointed behind log</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Log rate</td><td>Sample <code>wal_bytes</code>, <code>wal_fpi</code> (<code>pg_stat_wal</code>) or <code>pg_current_wal_lsn()</code></td><td>Sample <code>Innodb_os_log_written</code></td><td><code>SQLITE_DBSTATUS_CACHE_WRITE</code></td><td>A rise with unchanged traffic: new indexes, bulk changes, or more full-page images</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Log kept</td><td><code>pg_ls_waldir()</code>; <code>pg_stat_archiver</code>; <code>pg_replication_slots</code></td><td>Binary log directory size; <code>binlog_expire_logs_seconds</code></td><td><code>-wal</code> file size</td><td>Archiving failing, an abandoned slot, a reader that never ends</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Log flush</td><td><code>wal</code> rows of <code>pg_stat_io</code> (<code>fsyncs</code>, <code>fsync_time</code>, <code>write_time</code>); waits <code>WalSync</code>, <code>WalWrite</code></td><td>Sample <code>Innodb_os_log_fsyncs</code>; redo file I/O time in the Performance Schema</td><td>Commit time measured in the application</td><td>Flush time up with the same workload: storage is slower</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Old versions</td><td><code>n_dead_tup</code>, <code>last_autovacuum</code>; oldest <code>backend_xmin</code></td><td>History list length (<code>trx_rseg_history_len</code>); oldest <code>INNODB_TRX</code></td><td><code>-wal</code> size with checkpointed behind log</td><td>A long transaction or snapshot, or cleanup falling behind</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Recovery</td><td>Startup lines in the server log; progress every 10 s</td><td>Crash recovery and rollback messages in the error log</td><td><code>SQLITE_NOTICE_RECOVER_*</code> via <code>sqlite3_log()</code></td><td>A replay longer than your target; a large rollback after opening</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="essay-sources" aria-labelledby="sources"><h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
      <p>Checked on 17 October 2026. PostgreSQL outputs were recorded on a laptop with PostgreSQL 18.6; SQLite outputs with SQLite 3.54.0 through Python. No MySQL server was run; InnoDB names come from the MySQL 8.4 manual, and its messages from the 8.4 server source.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href={`${docs}monitoring-stats.html`}>The Cumulative Statistics System</a> defines every <code>pg_stat_*</code> view used here, including <code>pg_stat_io</code>’s rows for the log. <a href={`${docs}wal-configuration.html`}>WAL Configuration</a> explains checkpoint triggers, the warning and why log accumulates; <a href={`${docs}view-pg-replication-slots.html`}><code>pg_replication_slots</code></a> defines <code>wal_status</code>; <a href={`${docs}pgbuffercache.html`}>pg_buffercache</a> and <a href={`${docs}pgtestfsync.html`}>pg_test_fsync</a> are the two tools named.</li>
        <li>MySQL 8.4 Reference Manual: <a href={`${mysql}server-status-variables.html`}>Server Status Variables</a> for every <code>Innodb_</code> counter, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-standard-monitor.html`}>InnoDB Standard Monitor output</a> for <code>SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS</code>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool-flushing.html`}>Buffer Pool Flushing</a> and <a href={`${mysql}innodb-redo-log.html`}>Redo Log</a> for flushing under redo pressure, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-purge-configuration.html`}>Purge Configuration</a> for the history list, and <a href={`${mysql}innodb-recovery.html`}>InnoDB Recovery</a> for rollback after a crash. The server’s <a href="https://github.com/mysql/mysql-server/blob/8.4/share/messages_to_error_log.txt">error-log message file</a> on the 8.4 branch defines the recovery and redo messages quoted. PostgreSQL’s end-of-log messages are explained in <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/message-id/5031.1296689542%40sss.pgh.pa.us">Tom Lane’s 2011 reply on pgsql-admin</a>, and the checkpoint trigger rule in <a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres/blob/REL_18_STABLE/src/backend/access/transam/xlog.c">xlog.c</a>.</li>
        <li>SQLite: <a href={`${sqlite}wal.html`}>Write-Ahead Logging</a>, especially “Avoiding Excessively Large WAL Files”; <a href={`${sqlite}pragma.html#pragma_wal_checkpoint`}>PRAGMA wal_checkpoint</a> for the three result columns; <a href={`${sqlite}c3ref/c_dbstatus_options.html`}>sqlite3_db_status options</a> for the cache counters; <a href={`${sqlite}rescode.html`}>result codes</a> for the recovery notices.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/">Storage and durability</a>: the mechanisms behind every signal here.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/buffer-pool/">The buffer pool</a>: sizing the cache, what it keeps and evicts, and warming it after a restart.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/">Understanding database performance</a>: when the time goes to one query, a lock or the application rather than storage.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/">Row versions, transactions, and vacuum</a>: diagnosing retained snapshots and vacuum in depth.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/replication/">Following a change to another PostgreSQL server</a>: slots and the log they retain.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/">WAL, checkpoints, and recovery</a>: log positions, full-page images and a crash test on PostgreSQL 18.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">Backups and recovery</a>: what an incomplete archive costs when you restore.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
